NAME
    dump - display memory as hex, a page at a time

SYNOPSIS
    dump addr

DESCRIPTION
    Shows 256 bytes from the hexadecimal address addr as 16 rows of

        AAAA: bb bb ... bb  cccccccccccccccc

    (the address, 16 bytes in hex, then the same bytes as text with
    anything unprintable shown as "."), then waits for a key, read
    without echo: ".", q or Q, or Ctrl-D returns to the shell; any
    other key shows the next 256 bytes. "dump: bad address" when addr
    is not hex. dump is /BIN/DUMP.

EXAMPLES
    dump 6000      the program area, above dump itself
    dump 1000      the start of the OS
    dump 0F40      ARGBUF: this very command's argument tail, "0F40"

NOTES
    dump only reads memory; dep and examine change it. It runs from
    $5000, so dump 5000 shows dump's own code (look at a loaded program
    there from the monitor instead: exit, then B 5000). On the
    instruction-level emulator a lower-case q is read as end of input,
    which quits as well.
    Changed from the P8X version: q and Ctrl-D quit as well as ".";
    rows end in LF only.
    Ported from P8X os/commands/dump.c 2026-09-23.

SEE ALSO
    dep, examine, load, mon
